 except json.JSONDecodeError as error:\nscripts/verify_phase0_apply.py-74-        raise VerificationError(f\"invalid JSON in {sidecar_file}: {error}\") from error\nscripts/verify_phase0_apply.py-75-\nscripts/verify_phase0_apply.py:76:    if sidecar.get(\"schema_version\") != 1:\nscripts/verify_phase0_apply.py:77:        _fail(\"sidecar schema_version is not 1\")\nscripts/verify_phase0_apply.py-78-    config = sidecar.get(\"config\")\nscripts/verify_phase0_apply.py-79-    if not isinstance(config, dict):\nscripts/verify_phase0_apply.py-80-        _fail(\"sidecar config is missing or not an object\")\n--\nREADME.md-39-\nREADME.md-40-`vgt analyze [project.rpp]` requires a `.vgt` sidecar already written by the\nREADME.md-41-apply action above. It resolves the reference track's source audio file,\nREADME.md:42:runs the tempo/key/sections/chords detectors, and writes the results back\nREADME.md-43-into the sidecar as schema version 2 — never inside REAPER itself.\nREADME.md-44-\nREADME.md-45-Each detector's output is cached against a hash of the source audio and the\n--\nREADME.md-47-changed. Pass `--force` to recompute every stage regardless of the cache —\nREADME.md-48-useful after changing a detector's code rather than its inputs. Any stage can\nREADME.md-49-be corrected by hand-editing its `value` in the sidecar and setting\nREADME.md:50:`human_verified: true`; `vgt analyze` then leaves that stage untouched on\nREADME.md-51-every later run, regardless of what the audio hash says — even under `--force`.\nREADME.md-52-\nREADME.md-53-Progress is reported to stderr as each detector starts (the JSON result stays\n--\nREADME.md-67-- **Fallback: librosa's `beat_track`** (installed by default) when madmom\nREADME.md-68-  isn't installed, or fails to import. It gives beat times but no\nREADME.md-69-  downbeats, so time signature falls back to a `time_signature_hint`\nREADME.md:70:  setting (or `4/4`) rather than a detected bar length.\nREADME.md-71-\nREADME.md-72-Every run also renders a **click-only artifact** — `<project>.vgt-tempo-click.wav`,\nREADME.md:73:next to the sidecar — a bare click on the detected beat grid (the reference\nREADME.md-74-audio is not mixed in) so the grid can be checked by ear or lined up against\nREADME.md-75-the reference in REAPER; it is vgt-owned and regenerated each run, not\nREADME.md-76-committed alongside the project.\nREADME.md:77:The tempo stage's `value` also stores the raw detected `beat_times` — the\nREADME.md:78:shared beat-synchronous grid the `chords` stage below aligns to.\nREADME.md-79-\nREADME.md:80:### Key & beat-aligned chords\nREADME.md-81-\nREADME.md-82-The `key` stage detects root + scale (major/minor) from the reference\nREADME.md-83-track's full mix:\n--\nREADME.md-88-- **Fallback: librosa chroma** correlated against the 24 Krumhansl–Schmuckler\nREADME.md-89-  major/minor key templates — always available.\nREADME.md-90-\nREADME.md:91:The `chords` stage detects a beat-aligned maj/min chord sequence, with every\nREADME.md-92-segment boundary snapped onto the `tempo` stage's shared `beat_times` grid\nREADME.md:93:(not a separately detected grid):\nREADME.md-94-\nREADME.md-95-- **Primary backend: madmom**'s CNN chord recognizer, via the same `madmom`\nREADME.md-96-  extra as the tempo stage.\n--\nREADME.md-107-`\"vocabulary\": \"maj_min\"`.\nREADME.md-108-\nREADME.md-109-Every run also renders a **chord-sheet artifact** —\nREADME.md:110:`<project>.vgt-chords.txt`, next to the sidecar — a plain-text timestamp +\nREADME.md-111-chord-label listing for by-eye verification.\nREADME.md-112-\nREADME.md:113:The `chords` stage stores two parallel chord lists: `value` (the effective,\nREADME.md:114:human-correctable chords used everywhere else — apply, the chord sheet) and\nREADME.md:115:`detected` (the pristine machine detection, untouched by corrections). With\nREADME.md:116:no corrections the two are equal. `detected` is refreshed by `vgt analyze`\nREADME.md-117-in lockstep with `value`; once `value` is human-verified it freezes, and\nREADME.md:118:`detected` freezes alongside it, preserving exactly the detection a\nREADME.md-119-correction was made from. Keeping both around is what makes a future\nREADME.md-120-\"restore the original detection over this time range\" action possible.\nREADME.md-121-\n--\nREADME.md-140-\nREADME.md-141-Labels are intentionally generic — renaming them (`\"A\"` → `\"chorus\"`) is a\nREADME.md-142-normal correction, not a failure, and (like every other stage) persists once\nREADME.md:143:`human_verified: true` is set. Every run also renders a **section-timeline\nREADME.md-144-artifact** — `<project>.vgt-sections.txt`, next to the sidecar — so the\nREADME.md:145:detected structure can be checked by eye; it is vgt-owned and regenerated\nREADME.md-146-each run, not committed alongside the project.\nREADME.md-147-\nREADME.md-148-## Apply analysis (Phase 1)\nREADME.md-149-\nREADME.md-150-Run the same ReaScript again after `vgt analyze`. It reads the sidecar and adds\nREADME.md-151-`[vgt]` section regions plus a muted `[vgt] Chords` track whose text items\nREADME.md:152:carry the detected beat-aligned labels. Unlike every other vgt-owned label\nREADME.md-153-track, chord items are left **unlocked** — they are the editing surface for\nREADME.md-154-chord corrections (see below). It sets vgt-owned audio items to time-based\nREADME.md-155-positioning before any tempo changes.\nREADME.md-156-\nREADME.md-157-When REAPER still has exactly its single default 120 BPM / 4/4 tempo marker,\nREADME.md:158:the action writes the detected tempo map. If the project already has any other\nREADME.md-159-tempo map, it leaves that map untouched and instead creates a muted, locked\nREADME.md-160-`[vgt] Beats` item track. Both the regions and vgt tracks are reconciled on\nREADME.md-161-re-apply; no non-`[vgt]` track, region, or item is changed.\n--\nREADME.md-171-existing-map branches, each applied twice), use `--run-live` with the same\nREADME.md-172-baseline argument instead of supplying a project path.\nREADME.md-173-\nREADME.md:174:## Correcting chords in REAPER\nREADME.md-175-\nREADME.md:176:Auto-detected chords are sometimes wrong, and correcting raw second-offsets\nREADME.md:177:by hand in the sidecar is awkward. Instead, edit the chords directly on the\nREADME.md-178-`[vgt] Chords` track's timeline — its items are unlocked (though still muted,\nREADME.md-179-so they never play):\nREADME.md-180-\n--\nREADME.md-184-- **delete** an item to drop a spurious chord, or **add** a new item (with a\nREADME.md-185-  take name) to insert one.\nREADME.md-186-\nREADME.md:187:When done, load [reascript/vgt_read_chords.lua](reascript/vgt_read_chords.lua)\nREADME.md:188:in REAPER's Action List and run it (or run `vgt read-chords [project.rpp]`\nREADME.md-189-for the same pointer from the CLI). It scans the `[vgt] Chords` track's items\nREADME.md-190-— position, length, take name — by GUID against the sidecar's\nREADME.md-191-`managed_track_guids`, so it only ever reads objects vgt itself created, and\nREADME.md:192:writes them back into the sidecar as `analysis.chords.value.segments` with\nREADME.md:193:`chords.human_verified: true`. It never touches `analysis.chords.detected` —\nREADME.md-194-the original machine detection stays available there — nor any other\nREADME.md-195-analysis stage (tempo, key, sections) or the rest of the sidecar.\nREADME.md-196-\nREADME.md:197:From then on: `vgt analyze` skips the chords stage (human-verified stages are\nREADME.md-198-never recomputed), and re-running the apply ReaScript repaints `[vgt] Chords`\nREADME.md-199-identically from the corrected sidecar — the round trip is idempotent.\n--\nsrc/vgt/chords.py-8-match -- so every result is flagged `\"vocabulary\": \"maj_min\"`.\nsrc/vgt/chords.py-9-\nsrc/vgt/chords.py-10-Beat-alignment: chord segment boundaries are always snapped to the shared\nsrc/vgt/chords.py:11:beat grid detected by the tempo stage (tempo.py, #8) -- `detect_chords` take